      "body": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?><feed\n\txmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom\"\n\txmlns:thr=\"http://purl.org/syndication/thread/1.0\"\n\txml:lang=\"en-US\"\n\t>\n\t<title type=\"text\">AI | The Verge</title>\n\t<subtitle type=\"text\">The Verge is about technology and how it makes us feel. Founded in 2011, we offer our audience everything from breaking news to reviews to award-winning features and investigations, on our site, in video, and in podcasts.</subtitle>\n\n\t<updated>2026-10-28T16:32:41+00:00</updated>\n\n\t<link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" href=\"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence\" />\n\t<id>https://www.theverge.com/rss/ai-artificial-intelligence/index.xml</id>\n\t<link rel=\"self\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" href=\"https://www.theverge.com/rss/ai-artificial-intelligence/index.xml\" />\n\n\t<icon>https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/01/verge-rss-large_80b47e.png?w=150&amp;h=150&amp;crop=1</icon>\n\t\t<entry>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t<name>Andrew Liszewski</name>\n\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t<title type=\"html\"><![CDATA[Chrome’s enhanced autofill can now pull info from Gmail to help you fill out forms]]></title>\n\t\t\t<link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" href=\"https://www.theverge.com/tech/1020626/chrome-enhanced-autofill-gmail-forms-gemini-personal-intelligence\" />\n\t\t\t<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1020626</id>\n\t\t\t<updated>2026-10-28T12:32:41-04:00</updated>\n\t\t\t<published>2026-10-28T13:00:00-04:00</published>\n\t\t\t<category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"AI\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Chrome\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Google\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"News\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Tech\" />\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<summary type=\"html\"><![CDATA[Following improvements made to Chrome's enhanced autofill last year that included your name, passport number, email address, and physical addresses sourced from your Google Accounts and services like Google Wallet, the feature is now expanding. Starting today, Chrome's enhanced autofill will use Gemini with Personal Intelligence to suggest information sourced from your Gmail, potentially saving [&#8230;]]]></summary>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<content type=\"html\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<![CDATA[\n\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\n<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"A screenshot showing a form in Google Chrome with Gemini suggesting a piece of information sourced from Gmail.\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"Image: Google\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/chrome.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Following improvements made to Chrome's enhanced autofill last year that included your name, <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/news/812425/chrome-can-now-autofill-your-passport-number\">passport number</a>, email address, and physical addresses <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/news/838987/google-chrome-android-ios-desktop-autofill-account-wallet\">sourced from your Google Accounts</a> and services like Google Wallet, the feature is now expanding. Starting today, Chrome's enhanced autofill will use Gemini with Personal Intelligence to suggest information sourced from your Gmail, potentially saving you from having to search for confirmation emails, receipts, or shipping updates yourself.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The expanded autofill capabilities can retrieve details such as a shipment's tracking number, travel details like a flight booking number or hotel reservation, …</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/tech/1020626/chrome-enhanced-autofill-gmail-forms-gemini-personal-intelligence\">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t]]>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</content>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t</entry>\n\t\t\t<entry>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t<name>Emma Roth</name>\n\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t<title type=\"html\"><![CDATA[Meta says it&#8217;ll probably delay its controversial new business terms again]]></title>\n\t\t\t<link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" href=\"https://www.theverge.com/tech/1020769/meta-updated-business-terms-delay-indefinitely\" />\n\t\t\t<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1020769</id>\n\t\t\t<updated>2026-10-28T12:15:30-04:00</updated>\n\t\t\t<published>2026-10-28T12:15:30-04:00</published>\n\t\t\t<category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"AI\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Meta\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"News\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Policy\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Tech\" />\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<summary type=\"html\"><![CDATA[Meta says it may push the implementation of its new business terms past November 20th as it faces backlash from publishers over concerns about AI training. In a post on X, Meta communications head Andy Stone said the company does \"not plan to make policy changes without a full consultation with industry groups and leaders.\" [&#8230;]]]></summary>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<content type=\"html\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<![CDATA[\n\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\n<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"An image of the Meta logo on a red background\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"Illustration: Nick Barclay / The Verge\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/02/STK043_VRG_Illo_N_Barclay_3_Meta.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Meta says it may push the implementation of its new business terms past November 20th as it faces backlash from publishers over concerns about AI training. In <a href=\"https://x.com/andymstone/status/2115470598809735602?s=20\">a post on X</a>, Meta communications head Andy Stone said the company does \"not plan to make policy changes without a full consultation with industry groups and leaders.\"</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The new business terms, which were supposed to take effect on October 30th, initially <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/tech/1012846/meta-business-terms-scrape-news-publishers\">appeared to give Meta permission</a> to train its AI models on the news sites that use Facebook and Instagram. Meta later changed its policies to remove language indicating that it could \"train\" its products on content scraped from these si …</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/tech/1020769/meta-updated-business-terms-delay-indefinitely\">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t]]>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</content>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t</entry>\n\t\t\t<entry>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t<name>Emma Roth</name>\n\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t<title type=\"html\"><![CDATA[xAI is buying out homeowners who promise not to sue over data center noise]]></title>\n\t\t\t<link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/html\" href=\"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1020596/xai-mississippi-neighborhood-data-center-lawsuit\" />\n\t\t\t<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1020596</id>\n\t\t\t<updated>2026-10-28T10:44:00-04:00</updated>\n\t\t\t<published>2026-10-28T10:44:00-04:00</published>\n\t\t\t<category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"AI\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Elon Musk\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"News\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"Tech\" /><category scheme=\"https://www.theverge.com\" term=\"xAI\" />\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<summary type=\"html\"><![CDATA[A subsidiary of Elon Musk's xAI is offering to buy up homes in a Mississippi neighborhood, as long as residents don't join a lawsuit targeting the company's nearby data center and power plant, according to a report from The Guardian. A realtor going door-to-door in the Southaven neighborhood reportedly told one resident that xAI plans [&#8230;]]]></summary>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<content type=\"html\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<![CDATA[\n\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\n<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"A photo of xAI’s Southaven, Mississippi data center\" data-caption=\"xAI’s Southaven, Mississippi data center. | Photo by Brandon Dill / AFP via Getty Images\" data-portal-copyright=\"Photo by Brandon Dill / AFP via Getty Images\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/gettyimages-2289399619.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\txAI’s Southaven, Mississippi data center. | Photo by Brandon Dill / AFP via Getty Images\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A subsidiary of Elon Musk's xAI is offering to buy up homes in a Mississippi neighborhood, as long as residents don't join a lawsuit targeting the company's nearby data center and power plant, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/28/datacenter-noise-lawsuit-elon-musk-mississippi\">according to a report from <em>The Guardian</em></a>. A realtor going door-to-door in the Southaven neighborhood reportedly told one resident that xAI plans to purchase 418 homes, adding that 18 homeowners already have plans to sell. People who rent homes in the area report <a href=\"https://wreg.com/news/local/xai-memphis/spacexai-data-center-expansion-in-southaven-forces-renters-out/\">they're already being forced out</a>, with 60 days' notice after their landlords agreed to sell.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A recent report from the <a href=\"https://www.mississippifreepress.org/xai-moves-to-buy-out-southaven-residents-who-agree-not-to-sue-it-over-power-plant-noise-letter-shows/\"><em>Mississippi Free Press</em></a> includes a letter received by several households rec …</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1020596/xai-mississippi-neighborhood-data-center-lawsuit\">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t]]>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</content>\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t</entry>\n\t\t\t\n\t</feed>\n"
    }
  ]
}
